WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.216
An agent combines several small ideas.

00:00:03.216 --> 00:00:10.053
It reads a request, decides which fact is missing, uses a permitted tool, and checks the result.

00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:15.027
Memory helps it carry useful details from one turn into the next.

00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:20.513
You can understand a whole project by following one question all the way through.

00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:28.665
Ask what was known at the start, what was learned along the way, and which piece of evidence supports the final reply.

00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:32.646
Your next learning project can grow out of one weak spot.

00:00:32.646 --> 00:00:37.417
A bad document search calls for practice finding better passages.

00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:42.485
Unpredictable results across many tickets call for better tests.

00:00:42.485 --> 00:00:46.102
Return to Ana, who wants to know where her tent is.

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:50.009
The shop’s first helper could repeat a shipping policy.

00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:53.397
It could not tell her what happened to her own order.

00:00:53.397 --> 00:00:59.141
In your completed project, give Scout the same question and Ana’s order number.

00:00:59.141 --> 00:01:03.146
Write down the answer you expect from a known test record.

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:06.073
Everything you built turns around one loop.

00:01:06.073 --> 00:01:12.219
Scout reads the customer's question, together with what it remembers from the conversation.

00:01:12.219 --> 00:01:17.140
It plans: it works out what it needs to know, and which tool could tell it.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.804
It acts by calling a tool, such as get order or track parcel.

00:01:21.804 --> 00:01:27.854
It observes the result, including any error, and decides whether it knows enough yet.

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:33.701
Finally it answers within the policy and its limits, or hands over to a person.

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:39.887
This is the reason and act loop you can now recognise when you look inside Scout.

00:01:39.887 --> 00:01:41.446
Follow the run.

00:01:41.446 --> 00:01:48.358
The conversation supplies the number, Scout chooses the order tool, and the tool returns a status.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:52.448
The application checks what information it may share.

00:01:52.448 --> 00:01:56.813
Compare the reply with the actual status and tracking number.

00:01:56.813 --> 00:02:04.740
That comparison tests whether the parts worked together, not just whether the final sentence sounded friendly.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.286
Imagine you have one afternoon to improve Scout.

00:02:08.286 --> 00:02:11.203
Pick an actual failed question first.

00:02:11.203 --> 00:02:20.142
If Scout fetched the wrong passage for send it back, inspect retrieval before replacing the model or building another specialist.

00:02:20.142 --> 00:02:25.593
Save the original question and the policy passage a correct answer needs.

00:02:25.593 --> 00:02:30.196
Try one change to the search and repeat that exact enquiry.

00:02:30.196 --> 00:02:36.288
A new answer that sounds friendlier is not evidence that search found the right passage.

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:40.878
Finally ask a second version of the same customer question.

00:02:40.878 --> 00:02:45.991
If both now retrieve the right source, save the cases for the next change.

00:02:45.991 --> 00:02:53.584
You have turned a broad idea like learn retrieval into a small project with observable results.

00:02:53.584 --> 00:02:57.034
Change the test so the record cannot be found.

00:02:57.034 --> 00:03:03.937
A useful assistant asks for clarification or hands over instead of inventing a status.

00:03:03.937 --> 00:03:05.768
Keep both tests.

00:03:05.768 --> 00:03:13.462
If either fails, practice the specific skill the trace points to, fix one thing, and replay the same request.

00:03:13.462 --> 00:03:17.422
That gives you a concrete next step after this course.

00:03:17.422 --> 00:03:22.024
Where next? Here are four directions that build directly on Scout.

00:03:22.024 --> 00:03:32.308
Evaluations at scale: grow your ten test cases into hundreds, and use a model as a grader for answers that have no single right wording.

00:03:32.308 --> 00:03:42.616
Retrieval with embeddings: instead of matching words, search Orbit's documents by meaning, with a vector store such as Chroma or pgvector.

00:03:42.616 --> 00:03:55.140
More tools through MCP, the Model Context Protocol, an open standard for plugging an agent into ready-made tool servers, such as a help desk or a calendar.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:00.548
And multi-agent systems, for the day one agent is no longer enough.

00:04:00.548 --> 00:04:10.823
Everything you learned carries over to other providers, such as OpenAI, Google or open models; only the SDK calls change.

00:04:10.823 --> 00:04:16.871
Pick the direction that solves your next real problem, not the most exciting one.
